306 0 obj
<<
/Title (I am a member of the Terrapin Rocket Team at the University of Maryland. I am on the aerostructures subteam, where my work spans across multiple different areas. I use CAD and FEA to analyze and design structural components. Furthermore, I develop testing parameters to validate structures and inform design decisions between different compositions. I participate in running these tests and interpreting their results.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 257 0 R
/Next 307 0 R
>>
endobj
307 0 obj
<<
/Title (I also use RocketPy and OpenRocket to help simulate conditions and analyze performance. This helps with verifying that the rocket will be able to reach its targeted launch altitude of 30,000 feet, as well as to independently analyze specific parts of the rocket under their respective stresses. Our team meets weekly for performance updates and task delegation.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 257 0 R
/Prev 306 0 R
>>
endobj
308 0 obj
<<
/Title (As mechanical sub-team lead, I owned the sample-collection mechanism end to end. I designed an arm driven by servos and linear actuators that inserted into the material bed, opened to scoop its contents, and closed to retain the sample, then used a load cell to identify the correct material by measured weight.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 199 0 R
/Prev 258 0 R
/Next 309 0 R
>>
endobj
309 0 obj
<<
/Title (I modeled the mechanism and packaging via CAD, using assemblies to resolve fitment, mounting, and interface constraints across the drive system, sensors, and mission hardware, and refined the design through 3D-printed revisions and repeated testing across hundreds of iterations.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 199 0 R
/Prev 308 0 R
/Next 259 0 R
>>
endobj
310 0 obj
<<
/Title (I am experienced with and have coursework in Python, Numpy, and other methods of data analysis.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 128 0 R
/Prev 260 0 R
/Next 335 0 R
>>
endobj
